---
// Lesson 10.2b — ScriptableObjects: Card Data as Assets
// Card jam: the card becomes an ASSET — a file you create in the Project
// window, fill in the Inspector, and drag into a list.
//
// PROJECT: this lesson runs on Card-Game-Template-main (Unity 6000.4.0f1),
// which is NOT AP_CS_P_Starter_Project (Units 1-9). Nothing it names —
// `GameManager.cs`, `Card.cs`, `Card_data.cs`, `DraggableUI.cs`,
// `Assets/Cards/Card_Data/` — exists in the Units 1-9 project at all, so
// checking these claims against that project makes a true lesson look
// fabricated. Read them against the card template.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (re-read out of Card-Game-Template-main in this pass):
//   - Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs, 16 lines, quoted byte-for-byte in the
//     Concept Lab and taken apart line by line in the code half:
//     `[CreateAssetMenu(fileName = "Card_data", menuName = "Cards/Card_data",
//     order = 1)]`, `public class Card_data : ScriptableObject`, fields
//     card_name / description / health / cost / damage / sprite, and
//     `public int fontSize = 36;`. `fileName` is why a new asset arrives
//     called `Card_data.asset` until it is renamed; `menuName` is why the
//     menu entry is Cards ▸ Card_data and not something Unity guessed.
//   - Assets/Scripts/Card.cs, 54 lines: `public Card_data data;`, its own
//     copies of the same six value fields, five TextMeshProUGUI references
//     and two Images, and a `Start()` that assigns every one of them from
//     `data.*`. `healthText.text = health.ToString();` — the ints are
//     converted, the strings are not. It ends with a hardcoded
//     `if (card_name == "Orc") background.color = Color.chocolate;`, and the
//     Orc asset in this project really is named "Orc", so the branch is
//     reachable. `Card.cs` starts with `using TMPro;`, which is where
//     TextMeshProUGUI comes from.
//   - Assets/Scripts/GameManager.cs, 81 lines: the singleton
//     (`public static GameManager gm;` set in `Awake()` behind a duplicate
//     guard, then `DontDestroyOnLoad`) — the same shape as 5.3's StatManager.
//     SIX Lists: `player_deck`/`ai_deck` are `List<Card_data>`,
//     `player_hand`/`ai_hand`/`player_discard_pile`/`ai_discard_pile` are
//     `List<Card>`. `Deal()` is real: it shuffles both decks, instantiates
//     `blank_card` ONCE per side at the hand locations, assigns
//     `GetComponent<Card>().data`, and then disables `DraggableUI` on the
//     PLAYER's card (`new_card`), leaving it enabled on the AI's. Both
//     `Shuffle` overloads and `AI_Turn()` have empty bodies.
//     The deck list is `public`, so it is already authored in the Inspector:
//     no `[SerializeField]` line is needed and no `startingDeck` field exists
//     anywhere in the project. `Deal()` reads index `[0]` only, which is why
//     a stocked deck changes nothing visible yet.
//   - Assets/Cards/Card_Data/ ships exactly TWO card assets and they are the
//     lesson's best visual: `Orc_Data.asset` is filled in (card_name "Orc",
//     health 3, cost 2, damage 2, a sprite) and `Unicorn_data.asset` is blank
//     in every field. Their .asset files do not even carry the same set of
//     lines — Orc_Data has no `fontSize:` line — which is the point: the
//     Inspector ROWS come from the class, not from the file.
//   - Blank_Card.prefab is the `blank_card` GameManager instantiates. Its Card
//     component has all five TMP slots wired (Card_Name, Description, Health,
//     Cost, Damage) and two Images (Sprite, Background) — there is NO spare
//     text object, so a new text field means duplicating one with Ctrl+D.
//   - SampleScene contains Main Camera, `Dealer` (the object carrying
//     GameManager), an EventSystem and a Canvas — so UI dragging has the two
//     objects it needs, and `FindAnyObjectByType<Canvas>()` in Start() finds
//     one.
//   - CS0104, worth knowing before 10.2c: GameManager.cs line 3 is
//     `using Unity.Mathematics;` and line 4 is `using UnityEngine;`.
//     com.unity.mathematics 1.3.3 is installed transitively (absent from
//     Packages/manifest.json, pinned in Packages/packages-lock.json at
//     depth 2), and it declares its own `Unity.Mathematics.Random`, so a bare
//     `Random.Range(...)` in THAT file is an ambiguous reference and will not
//     compile. It must be written `UnityEngine.Random.Range(...)`, or fixed
//     with `using Random = UnityEngine.Random;`. No line in this lesson
//     writes `Random`, so it is recorded here rather than raised in the
//     walkthrough — but it is waiting in the file the shuffle goes into.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, editor/code split). Walkthrough step 4 was four
// `code:` lines of PURE EDITOR WORK — "Hierarchy ▸ open Blank_Card.prefab ▸
// select the Damage text object ▸ Ctrl+D", "the copy ▸ rename it Rarity",
// "Card component ▸ Rarity Text → drag the Rarity object in", "Play" — and
// the container was the wrong one twice over: it hid from every word count
// inside a code block, and it taught a student to type clicks as code. Those four
// clicks are now the pane-named "In the Editor" activity above the
// walkthrough, the walkthrough carries `label: 'In the Code'`, and the
// `finalFile`'s trailing `// Inspector changes made in this lesson:` block —
// a third copy of the same click work — is deleted. Every value that block
// carried is stated elsewhere on the page: `rarity` twice and `rarityText`
// in the code half; the duplicated text object and the wired slot in the
// editor steps; the card assets and the filled `player_deck` in Build Lab
// steps 2 and 3, which now names **Dealer** (the only fact the block held
// alone, and the real name of the object carrying `GameManager` in
// SampleScene).
//
// GROUND TRUTH corrected while moving it: the deleted `why` claimed "the
// TextMeshPro component on the copy still says 'Damage' in its Text field".
// It does not. All five TMP components in Blank_Card.prefab carry
// `m_text: New Text` — the object NAMES are Damage/Cost/Card_Name/
// Description/Health, the Text field was never filled on any of them. The
// step now says what is actually there.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION: Build Lab Step 1 restated the code half it follows
// (which file is taken apart, which field is mirrored where, and that both
// files already exist) and is now the pointer it was trying to be; the
// Concept Lab's "Make FIVE cards" was the same assignment as Build Lab Step 2
// and the lab keeps it; and the Concept Lab's "so read the file now" repeated
// its own "open Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs and read it" three lines above.
// Three stale "Build It With Me" pointers name the block by the label it now
// carries.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted: the Concept Lab's socratic
// block keeps its one question in place (10.2b:sc1:q1) and the decompose
// block keeps all three turns (dc1:q1-q3). The new editor block adds no
// socratic and therefore no id.
//
// SCAFFOLDING: FULL WALKTHROUGH (level 1) — a first introduction, and the
// first ScriptableObject anywhere in this course, so every line is given and
// every line is explained, with zero gaps, plus the finished file behind a
// <details>. verify-site refuses a level-1 lesson that
// withholds a line or leaves the answer key open, and refuses one that
// renders no walkthrough at all.
//
// GROUND TRUTH corrected in this pass (all three were defects in the old
// file, and two of them were flagged):
//   1. old Build Lab Step 1 said "build the card script above" — nothing
//      above showed a card script, and it contradicted the Concept Lab, which
//      correctly says the template already ships `Card_data.cs`. There is now
//      a real walkthrough above it, and Step 1 names the two files and the
//      four lines instead of pointing at air.
//   2. old Step 1 hedged "check whether your starter already has a Card.cs —
//      some versions ship one". There is one version of this project and it
//      definitely ships `Card.cs`; the hedge is gone. The teaching point
//      underneath it is kept and is now the spine of the walkthrough: a new
//      field is only useful when it is declared on BOTH `Card_data` (so the
//      asset can hold it) and `Card` (so `Start()` copies it and the card can
//      show it). Writing a class the template already ships is a CS0101
//      duplicate-definition error, so the lesson says READ AND EXTEND.
//   3. old Step 2 told the student to add
//      `[SerializeField] private List<Card> startingDeck;` to GameManager.
//      No such field exists, the deck is `List<Card_data>` and not
//      `List<Card>`, and no field is needed at all — `player_deck` is already
//      `public` and already in the Inspector. The fabricated snippet is
//      replaced by the real declaration.
//   4. the Concept Lab's data-homes table credited a `maxAttackRange` field to
//      3.1. 3.1 has no such field and neither does the Units 1-9 project; the
//      `[SerializeField]` reference 3.1 actually teaches is
//      `playerTransform`, so that is what the row says now.
