    <code>Orc_Data.asset</code> is filled in: a name, a description,
    health, cost, damage and a sprite. <code>Unicorn_data.asset</code>
    is empty in every single field — a card that was created and never
    written. Same class, same Inspector, two very different amounts of
    work.</p>

    <p>Now find the last thing in <code>Assets/Scripts/Card.cs</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>if (card_name == "Orc")
{
    background.color = Color.chocolate;
}</code></pre>
    <p>That is where an asset meets the code. The card's name is not a
    string somebody typed into a script — it is <code>data.card_name</code>,
    copied onto the card by <code>Start()</code>, and "Orc" is exactly what
    the Orc asset says. Rename that asset in the Inspector and this branch
    stops running. Data you can edit with the mouse just decided what the
    code does.</p>

    <p><strong>Compare your three data homes so far:</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Data lives in…</th><th>Example you built</th><th>Edited by</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Serialized fields on a component</td><td>the <code>playerTransform</code> reference wired in the Inspector (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)</td><td>Inspector, per scene object</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scene/prefab YAML</td><td>everything (<a href="9.4.html">9.4</a>)</td><td>Unity (and you, by hand, once)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ScriptableObject assets</td><td>your cards</td><td>Inspector, in the PROJECT, reusable everywhere</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The two deck Lists hold <code>Card_data</code> assets; the
            four hand and discard Lists hold <code>Card</code> objects.
            When you drag the SAME card asset into three of those
            Lists, how many copies of the card exist in memory — and
            why is "reference, not copy" exactly what
            <a href="9.4.html">9.4</a> guids do for sprites?
            (One card asset, many references. The exam's word is
            abstraction — again.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A new field can be declared in both files, stored in an asset and copied onto the card,
        and still be invisible — because nothing on the prefab is drawing it. This half is the
        object that draws it, and there is no spare one: every text object on the card is already
        wired to a line in the code half.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Cards/Card_Data/</code> ▸ double-click
            <strong>Blank_Card</strong> to open the prefab. This is the card
            <code>GameManager.Deal()</code> clones for each hand, and its five text objects are
            Card_Name, Description, Health, Cost and Damage.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> the <strong>Damage</strong> object ▸
            <strong>Ctrl+D</strong> (<strong>Cmd+D</strong> on a Mac) to duplicate it.
            Duplicating in place keeps the TextMeshPro font, size and anchors the card is laid
            out with, which is faster and more consistent than adding a fresh text object from
            the <strong>GameObject ▸ UI</strong> menu and setting all three by hand.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> rename the copy <strong>Rarity</strong>. It arrives
            still called Damage, and a Hierarchy holding two objects of the same name is a
            Hierarchy you cannot fill a slot from correctly.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the copy's <strong>TextMeshPro</strong> component ▸
            <strong>Text</strong> field, which still holds the placeholder <code>New Text</code>.
            Clear it: five labels reading the same word are five labels you cannot tell apart
            while you are placing one of them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> drag Rarity to where the value belongs on the card,
            clear of the other four labels. Two values drawn on top of each other is a card that
            looks broken with nothing wrong in the code.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half below is done — Inspector →</strong> the
            <strong>Card</strong> component on the prefab root ▸ <strong>Rarity Text</strong>,
            then drag the Rarity object in from the Hierarchy. That slot is the
            <code>rarityText</code> field you declare below, and it is the same
            drag-into-a-slot gesture as the sprite field and the deck list. Drag the <em>wrong</em>
            object in — the original Damage label — and two lines write to the same label, so
            whichever runs last wins and one of the two values quietly disappears.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play. The card draws the value you typed into a
            <em>file</em>. Not one of the four lines you added was needed to author a card — they
            were needed to add a field, once, for every card in the game.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the code half above is this step's work.</strong>
    If you have not done it yet, do it now: the rest of this lab assumes
    a card can show a field you invented.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — author five cards.</strong> In the Project window,
    right-click &rarr; Create &rarr; Cards &rarr; Card_data. The new file
    arrives called <code>Card_data</code>, so rename it to the card before
    you do anything else or your project fills up with
    <code>Card_data 1</code>, <code>Card_data 2</code> and no way to tell
    them apart in a deck list. Fill each one in the Inspector, keep them
    together in <code>Assets/Cards/Card_Data/</code> beside the two that
    ship, and give each card a number that makes it different from the
    others — a card that costs the same and hits the same as every other
    card is not a card, it is the same card wearing a different name. Five
    is today; a jam deck wants fifteen to twenty of them.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — put them in the deck.</strong> No new field, and no
    code. <code>GameManager.cs</code> already declares the list the
    Inspector edits:</p>
    <pre><code>public List&lt;Card_data&gt; player_deck = new List&lt;Card_data&gt;();</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select <strong>Dealer</strong> — the object
    carrying <code>GameManager</code> — and
    <code>player_deck</code> is waiting: an element count, then one slot per
    card. Drag your assets in. Duplicate entries are legal — three copies of
    your best card is a design decision that costs three slots and one asset
    file. Read the slots that are already there before you fill them: a slot
    showing <code>None</code> is a null reference sitting in the list, and
    <code>Deal()</code> reads <code>player_deck[0]</code> and hands whatever
    it finds straight to <code>Card.Start()</code>, which reads
    <code>data.card_name</code> on the first frame. An empty slot is not a
    harmless one.</p>

    <p>Then notice how little <code>Deal()</code> does with all that work:
    it instantiates one card per side and reads index <code>[0]</code>. A
    deck of twenty and a deck of one look identical on screen today — you
    are stocking a list that the shuffle in <a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>
    is going to need before it can mean anything.</p>

    <p><strong>And read one more line while you are in there.</strong> The
    last line of <code>Deal()</code> turns <code>DraggableUI</code> off on
    the player's card and leaves it on for the AI's. The drag behaviour
    ships with the project, and the scene ships the Canvas and EventSystem
    that UI dragging needs. Which card gets to be dragged, though, is a
    decision somebody made — read the line and decide whether it is a bug or
    a rule, and say which one you would have written.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — prove the pipeline.</strong> Add a card WITHOUT
    touching code: create, fill, drag into the deck, play. If that took
    under two minutes, your content pipeline works and the rest of the jam
    is content rather than plumbing. The proof is not that a new card
    exists — it is that you never opened a script to make it.</p>

</div>

